; cluster for everyday material properties.
; not chemistry — not molecular structure or atomic bonds.
; what anyone knows from touching, lifting, dropping, heating things.
; 
; the four properties anyone directly perceives:
; 
; phase       — solid, liquid, gas at everyday temperatures.
; water is liquid at room temperature.
; ice is solid below 0°C. steam is gas above 100°C.
; iron is solid. air is gas. mercury is liquid.
; bridges to: thermodynamics (phase-change, temperature)
; 
; elasticity  — how much a material bounces or returns to its shape.
; rubber is elastic (e ≈ 0.8). steel ball is very elastic (e ≈ 0.9).
; clay is inelastic (e ≈ 0). glass is brittle (shatters, not bounces).
; bridges to: physics (coefficient-of-restitution, spring-force)
; 
; hardness    — resistance to deformation under force.
; diamond is hard. rubber is soft. clay is deformable. water yields.
; a hard material transmits force; a soft material absorbs it.
; bridges to: physics (normal-force, pressure, compression)
; 
; thermal     — how a material responds to heat.
; metal conducts heat (feels cold to touch, carries heat fast).
; wood insulates (feels warm, carries heat slowly).
; water has high heat capacity (slow to heat, slow to cool).
; bridges to: thermodynamics (heat-transfer, temperature)
; 
; material properties are not absolute — they are relational and contextual.
; rubber is elastic wrt clay. rubber is inelastic wrt a steel spring.
; ice is solid at room temperature but liquid in the sun.
; the apeksha (reference condition) is always implicit in everyday speech.
; the kosha declares the default condition (room temperature, normal pressure).

(kosha materials-varga
  (vishesa common-sense-varga)
  (yukta physics thermodynamics chemistry))
